package pages

templ SyntaxDeclarationsPage() {
	<h1>Declarations</h1>
	<ul>
		<li>
			The assignment operator <code>=</code> can be used to assign an existing non-constant value
			<ul>
				<li>It is also used to assign a value to an explicitly typed variable in-line</li>
				<li>The operator can also be used to discard a value using the <code>_ = ...</code> syntax</li>
			</ul>
		</li>
		<li>
			You can discard expression results using the <code>_ = ...</code> syntax
			<ul>
				<li>In ghoti, identifiers cannot start with <code>_</code> and thus cannot be named <code>_</code></li>
			</ul>
		</li>
		<li>
			Leaving out the type annotation (<code>const x = value;</code>) infers the type from the assigned value
			<ul>
				<li>An unannotated declaration must be initialized with a value</li>
			</ul>
		</li>
		<li>
			There is no shadowing in ghoti
			<ul>
				<li>You cannot declare the same variable name in the same scope or enclosing scope</li>
			</ul>
		</li>
	</ul>
	<pre>
		<code class="language-ghoti">
			{ `const a = 2;          // Type deduced to be i32
let b = "str";        // Type deduced to be a constant size array of bytes (non-null terminated)
let c: u8 = 's';      // Explicitly typed, so value must agree
let d;                // Illegal, an unannotated declaration needs a value!
let mut e: []u8 = undefined; // Allowed, e is uninitialized and future assignments must be a slice of bytes` }
		</code>
	</pre>
	<h2>Binding Forms</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>Every declaration uses exactly one of <code>const</code>, <code>let</code>, or <code>let mut</code></li>
		<li>A declaration must be initialized with a value, unless it is <code>extern</code>; use <code>= undefined</code> to leave it uninitialized</li>
	</ul>
	<table>
		<thead>
			<tr><th>Form</th><th>Known</th><th>Mutable</th><th>Storage</th></tr>
		</thead>
		<tbody>
			<tr><td><code>const x</code></td><td>at compile time</td><td>no</td><td>only when its address is taken</td></tr>
			<tr><td><code>let x</code></td><td>at runtime</td><td>no</td><td>yes</td></tr>
			<tr><td><code>let mut x</code></td><td>at runtime</td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td></tr>
			<tr><td><code>comptime let mut x</code></td><td>at compile time</td><td>yes, while compiling</td><td>no</td></tr>
		</tbody>
	</table>
	<h2>Compile-time Constants</h2>
	<ul>
		<li><code>const</code> values must be computable at compile time; anything else is an error that suggests <code>let</code></li>
		<li>Types, functions, <code>undefined</code> aliases, and values that hold <code>type</code>s are always declared with <code>const</code></li>
		<li>Taking the address of a <code>const</code> (<code>^TABLE</code>) gives a pointer to one read-only copy</li>
		<li><code>comptime let mut</code> declares a local that changes while compiling, such as a cursor in a <code>{ "for comptime" }</code> loop; it has no address</li>
		<li>
			A <code>comptime let mut</code> can hold a type: each use names the type it holds at that point, and only another type can be assigned to it
			<ul>
				<li>A <code>{ "for comptime" }</code> or <code>{ "while comptime" }</code> loop can build a type up one iteration at a time</li>
			</ul>
		</li>
		<li><code>comptime let</code> without <code>mut</code> is an error: that is spelled <code>const</code></li>
	</ul>
	<pre>
		<code class="language-ghoti">
			{ `comptime let mut T: type = u8;
for comptime (0..3) |_| { T = Wrap(T); }
let nested: T = undefined;   // Wrap(Wrap(Wrap(u8)))` }
		</code>
	</pre>
	<h2>Runtime Values</h2>
	<ul>
		<li><code>let</code> declares a value that cannot be reassigned, and <code>let mut</code> one that can</li>
		<li>Neither has to be compile-time known, though a <code>let</code> whose value is still folds where a compile-time value is needed (<code>let n = 4; let a: [n]u8 = ...;</code>); a <code>let mut</code> never does</li>
		<li>A mutable reference cannot be taken from a <code>const</code> or a <code>let</code></li>
		<li>A closure that captures runtime state is declared with <code>let</code></li>
		<li>Reading a <code>let mut</code> that has not been assigned since <code>= undefined</code> is illegal behavior</li>
	</ul>
	<pre>
		<code class="language-ghoti">
			{ `const a = 1;       // Compile-time constant
let b = read();    // Runtime value, fixed once set
let mut c = b;     // Runtime value that can change
let d = &mut b;    // Illegal, cannot take mutable reference of an immutable value` }
		</code>
	</pre>
	<h2>Compile-time Parameters</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>A parameter written <code>comptime n: usize</code> must be passed a compile-time value, and the function is instantiated once per value</li>
		<li>
			A function declared with <code>const</code> also infers this: a parameter its body reads at compile time is compile-time at each call
			<ul>
				<li>The function stays callable at runtime as long as those parameters receive compile-time values</li>
				<li>Closures declared with <code>let</code> do not infer; mark their parameters <code>comptime</code> instead</li>
			</ul>
		</li>
	</ul>
	<pre>
		<code class="language-ghoti">
			{ `const min = fn(a: i32, b: i32): i32 {
    return if comptime (a < b) a else b; // a and b are inferred to be compile-time
};
const three = min(3, 9);` }
		</code>
	</pre>
	<h2>Modifiers</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>
			Declarations have many different modifiers that affect linkage, access, and ownership
			<ul>
				<li><code>pub</code>: When used inside of a struct, this opens access to non-struct-local functions (i.e. member functions). When used on a top-level declaration, this lets importing files access the declaration. The presence of this keyword correctly implies that all declarations are <em>private</em> unless explicitly stated otherwise.</li>
				<li><code>extern</code>: Denotes a declaration as relating to a symbol yet-to-be defined (i.e. external linkage). This currently supports only C symbols. This keyword cannot be combined with the <code>export</code> modifier.</li>
				<li><code>export</code>: Forwards the declaration to the 'outside world'. This means that the symbol is treated as a C symbol. This keyword cannot be combined with the <code>extern</code> modifier.</li>
				<li><code>weak</code> and <code>threadlocal</code>: a weak symbol may be overridden at link time; a thread-local one has a copy per thread</li>
			</ul>
		</li>
		<li>
			Linkage needs a symbol to attach to
			<ul>
				<li>Data with <code>extern</code>, <code>export</code>, <code>weak</code>, or <code>threadlocal</code> is declared with <code>let</code> or <code>let mut</code>, since a <code>const</code> has no storage</li>
				<li>A function with linkage is declared with <code>const</code>; the linkage applies to the function itself</li>
			</ul>
		</li>
	</ul>
	<pre>
		<code class="language-ghoti">
			{ `pub let mut c = 2;                     // Allowed, symbol can be imported
extern let errno: i32;                   // Allowed, externs must be explicitly typed without values
extern const puts: fn(s: ^u8): i32;      // Allowed, an extern function
export("counter") let mut counter = 1;  // Allowed
export const x = 1;                     // Illegal, a const has no symbol to export` }
		</code>
	</pre>
	<h2>Attributes</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>An <code>{ "@[...]" }</code> list before a declaration, function literal, or field adjusts how it is compiled</li>
		<li><code>discardable</code>: callers may ignore the function's result</li>
		<li><code>inline(.always | .never | .hint | .default)</code>: how calls to the function are inlined</li>
		<li><code>naked</code>: no prologue or epilogue; the body must be inline assembly</li>
		<li><code>align(n)</code>: minimum alignment of the storage or code, in bytes</li>
		<li><code>deprecated</code> or <code>deprecated("why")</code>: uses warn, or fail under <code>--deprecated=error</code></li>
		<li>
			<code>testing</code>: a test helper that may use 
			<code>
				{ "@expect" }
			</code>, <code>{ "@require" }</code>, and <code>{ "@skip" }</code> (see Testing Statements)
		</li>
		<li>
			<code>visibility(.default | .hidden | .protected)</code>: who outside the linked image sees the symbol
			<ul>
				<li>Only meaningful on a <code>pub</code>, <code>export</code>, <code>extern</code>, or <code>weak</code> declaration</li>
				<li><code>.hidden</code> keeps a symbol out of a shared library's exports while other objects in the same link still see it</li>
				<li><code>.protected</code> exports the symbol but never lets another library override it; it exists only for ELF targets</li>
			</ul>
		</li>
	</ul>
	<pre>
		<code class="language-ghoti">
			{ `@[visibility(.hidden)] export weak const __addtf3 = fn(a: f128, b: f128): f128 { ... };
@[inline(.always), align(16)] const fast = fn(x: i32): i32 { return x; };` }
		</code>
	</pre>
	<h2>Testing Statements</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>The <code>test</code> keyword is used to declare top-level unit test blocks that are compiled into test runner executables.</li>
		<li>Tests can optionally take a string literal description, or be declared without a description string.</li>
		<li>Test blocks contain standard statement blocks and are automatically executed by the test runner.</li>
		<li>
			The runner entry point is <code>test_runner(args: [][:0]u8, tests: []Test): i32</code>; it receives the process
			arguments (as <code>main</code> does) plus the discovered tests. A <code>weak</code> default is provided.
		</li>
		<li><code>{ "@expect" }(cond)</code> fails the test and keeps going, <code>{ "@require" }(cond)</code> fails it and stops, and <code>{ "@skip" }()</code> stops it without failing</li>
		<li>
			A function marked <code>{ "@[testing]" }</code> may use them too, so a check too involved for one expression can live in a helper
			<ul>
				<li>A <code>{ "@require" }</code> or <code>{ "@skip" }</code> in a helper stops the test that called it, through any helpers in between</li>
				<li>A helper is only called directly, from a test or another <code>{ "@[testing]" }</code> function</li>
			</ul>
		</li>
	</ul>
	<pre>
		<code class="language-ghoti">
			{ `test "addition logic" {
    let x = 1 + 1;
    _ = x;
}

test {
    let y = 2 + 2;
    _ = y;
}

@[testing]
const check_sum = fn(a: i32, b: i32, expected: i32): void {
    @require(a >= 0 and b >= 0, "negative input");
    @expect(a + b == expected);
};

test "sums" {
    check_sum(1, 2, 3);
    check_sum(40, 2, 42);
}` }
		</code>
	</pre>
	<h2>Assignment</h2>
	<ul>
		<li><code>let mut</code> declarations can be reassigned</li>
		<li>Re-assignment is right associative, meaning that you can chain assignment expressions:</li>
	</ul>
	<pre>
		<code class="language-ghoti">
			{ `let mut a: i32 = 2;
let mut b: i32 = 5;
a = (b = 6);` }
		</code>
	</pre>
	<ul>
		<li>This works because an assignment returns the assigned value</li>
	</ul>
	<h2>Type Aliasing</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>A type alias is an ordinary <code>const</code> declaration whose value is a type</li>
		<li>Aliases are resolved entirely at compile time and never occupy storage</li>
		<li>An alias can be marked public in order to expose it to the outside world when the enclosing file is imported</li>
	</ul>
	<pre>
		<code class="language-ghoti">
			{ `const MyBool = bool;               // Private to this file
pub const MyPublicBool = bool;     // Publicly accessible via namespacing when imported
const BytePtr = ^mut u8;           // Pointer, reference, array, and slice types work as values
const Callback = fn(status: i32): void;
const AnyWriter = &dyn Writer;
const IntList = List(i32);         // As does the result of a type constructor` }
		</code>
	</pre>
	<ul>
		<li>Note that this is an <em>alias</em>, so there is no difference between using the aliased type and handwriting the entire raw type</li>
		<li>Whether a declaration aliases a type or binds a value is decided by what its right-hand side denotes: <code>const T = ^i32;</code> is a type, while <code>const p = ^x;</code> is a pointer to the variable <code>x</code></li>
		<li>A type alias cannot be stored in a <code>let</code> or <code>let mut</code>, and naming a type where a value is expected is an error:</li>
	</ul>
	<pre>
		<code class="language-ghoti">
			{ `const P = ^i32;
let Q = ^i32;                      // Illegal: a type only exists at compile time
const p: ^i32 = P;                  // Illegal: 'P' is a type, not a '^i32' value` }
		</code>
	</pre>
	<ul>
		<li>
			The most common use cases for this are for saving keystrokes for long types and for passing complex types to functions at the call site
			<ul>
				<li>Since function calls prioritize expressions over types, an attempt to pass <code>&amp;mut P</code> will always be parsed as a mutable reference to an object P</li>
				<li>Declaring the type with <code>const T = &amp;mut P;</code> allows you to then pass the identifier to the function</li>
				<li>This is more explicit than having to go to the function definition to see what is being expected at the call site</li>
			</ul>
		</li>
		<li>
			You may declare types such as unions, enums, and structs this way, though they must be bare types without modifiers
			<ul>
				<li>This restriction is in place as it would be impossible to instantiate the underlying type</li>
			</ul>
		</li>
	</ul>
	<pre>
		<code class="language-ghoti">
			{ `const S = &struct { ... };        // Illegal
const S = ^union { ... };         // Illegal
const S = enum { ... };           // Legal` }
		</code>
	</pre>
}
